   For the sibling tests: waitForText('Connection status') — 'Connection status' is row 2. If the paint sequence is leading-row-first-then-rest (as observed: "the leading menu row paints one frame before the rest"), then by the time row 2 is in raw, is it on screen? The observed failure was: header + Stop DAP painted, sibling rows missing — that was waiting on 'Stop DAP' (row 1) and asserting rows 2-3. Waiting on row 2 ('Connection status') then arrow-walking: the arrows land; the picker is already open (row 2 painted means the picker frame with at least 2 rows is up). Row 2 in raw means row 2 was in a painted frame → picker is open → arrows go to the picker. Enter selects. The remaining rows (3-5) painting later doesn't affect selection. So those legs are substantially safer. But there's a subtler variant: a repaint could momentarily clear the screen (the about-test comment mentions "a late boot-frame repaint can overwrite the screen between two observations"). If arrows land during a cleared frame... keystrokes go to the TUI's input model, not the screen — the picker state persists across repaints. So arrow input isn't affected by paint state at all! The keyboard handling reads the model state (menuOpen), not the painted frame. me…

